<!--
  A test rig for the check after pickup (src/held_module_finder.h), without
  the arm.

  The tool (models/tool.xml) is mounted by an include inside a body, as the
  arm mounts it, but on a carriage instead of the arm: rig_carriage, a mocap
  body, which a test puts exactly where it wants by writing mocap_pos and
  mocap_quat. Its frame is the flange's: +Z out of the flange, toward the
  module. As built it points straight down, the tool's X along world +X, so
  tool_tip is at (0, 0, 1.585) with its +Z pointing down. Being mocap it
  does not give under the module's weight: its pose is known exactly, as the
  arm's tool tip is known from the joint angles.

  module_0, 2.3 m x 1.1 m x 0.035 m and 30 kg, hangs under the tool, glass
  up, its long side along world X, held by the real suction weld
  (models/suction.xml, src/suction.h): a test teleports it to the grip
  offset it wants and calls Suction::Grip, which holds it there. As built
  it is at the design grip, the cups on the middle of its glass: its
  underside at 1.55 m. The cups and the module do not collide here
  (<contact> below): a grip offset 10 mm "into" the glass or tilted 1.5
  degrees would otherwise put the end cups through the glass and push the
  module off the pose the test chose; on the real tool the cups' bellows
  give.

  check_camera stands on the floor under the module and looks straight up.
  Why there and so: see the comment at the camera.

  rig_ceiling, a mocap plate 4 m up, is a roof over the rig: with it every
  pixel beyond the module's edges reads something, so with the noise model
  on every edge has flying pixels as well as dropped ones. Moved away (a
  test sets its mocap_pos far off), the camera sees nothing beyond the
  edges and they only drop out.

  module_1, of another size (2.0 m x 1.0 m), lies on the floor out of view
  until a test grips it in module_0's place.

  Everything in this file other than the modules and the camera is named
  rig_.
-->
<mujoco model="check scene">
  <include file="common.xml"/>
  <include file="suction.xml"/>

  <default>
    <!-- The carriage, the roof and the camera's housing are for looking at
         only: nothing collides with them. -->
    <default class="rig">
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" rgba="0.5 0.55 0.6 1"/>
    </default>
    <default class="module">
      <geom type="box" size="1.15 0.55 0.0175" mass="30" friction="0.8 0.005 0.0001"
            rgba="0.1 0.15 0.35 1"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- The carriage: the flange 0.30 m above tool_tip, pointing down. -->
    <body name="rig_carriage" mocap="true" pos="0 0 1.885" xyaxes="1 0 0 0 -1 0"
          childclass="rig">
      <geom name="rig_carriage_block" type="box" size="0.12 0.12 0.1" pos="0 0 -0.1"/>
      <!-- The tool goes back to the default class, so that it is as it is
           on the arm. -->
      <body name="rig_mount" childclass="main">
        <include file="tool.xml"/>
      </body>
    </body>

    <body name="rig_ceiling" mocap="true" pos="0 0 4.0" childclass="rig">
      <geom name="rig_ceiling" type="box" size="4 4 0.05" rgba="0.8 0.8 0.78 1"/>
    </body>

    <!--
      The check camera. MuJoCo's camera looks along its own -Z with +Y up
      in the image, so looking straight up is -Z = world +Z; here its +X
      (the image's rows) is world +X turned 5 degrees about the vertical,
      and its +Y is world -Y turned the same.

      Where and how wide: the module's underside 1.40 m above the lens
      (underside at 1.55 m, lens at 0.15 m). The underside has to be in view
      whole, with room for the module being up to 5 cm and 3 degrees off
      the design grip: its corners then reach 0.117 m further out, so 2.53
      m x 1.33 m, which turned 5 degrees in the image needs 2.64 m x 1.55
      m. fovy 61 degrees with 848 x 480 square pixels gives a focal length
      of 240 / tan(30.5 deg) = 407.5 pixels and a horizontal field of 92.2
      degrees: 2.91 m x 1.65 m at the underside, 3.43 mm per pixel there,
      about 215,000 pixels on the underside. Nearer and wider would give
      finer pixels but a lens no real depth camera has; higher would take
      the module above where the carry passes anyway (over the pallet the
      script holds it with its underside at 1.565 m).

      Why turned 5 degrees: square-on, a pixel grid whose rows run exactly
      along an edge leaves that edge anywhere in the gap beyond the last
      row, up to half a pixel either way, which FitRectangle's dot counting
      cannot see (src/rectangle_fit.h). Turned, each long side crosses the
      rows about 60 times and the counting averages that out.

      Its housing is below the lens, wholly behind it, so it is never in the
      picture.
    -->
    <geom name="rig_camera_housing" class="rig" type="box" size="0.06 0.045 0.06"
          pos="0 0 0.07" rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1"/>
    <camera name="check_camera" pos="0 0 0.15"
            xyaxes="0.9961947 0.0871557 0 0.0871557 -0.9961947 0"
            fovy="61" resolution="848 480"/>

    <!-- At the design grip under the tool. -->
    <body name="module_0" pos="0 0 1.5675">
      <freejoint name="module_0"/>
      <geom name="module_0" class="module"/>
    </body>

    <!-- A module of another product, 2.0 m x 1.0 m, lying on the floor far
         out of the camera's view until a test grips it instead: the wrong
         size. -->
    <body name="module_1" pos="20 3 0.0175">
      <freejoint name="module_1"/>
      <geom name="module_1" class="module" size="1.0 0.5 0.0175" mass="26"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <contact>
    <exclude body1="tool" body2="module_0"/>
    <exclude body1="tool" body2="module_1"/>
  </contact>
</mujoco>
